Data   : two shared S8 reads via ImpersonationStore (web useImpersonation domain), bound through UsersPageViewModel
         over asUsersPageSource(); no HTTP in the view.
           - candidates (GET /admin/impersonate/candidates ▸ web useImpersonationCandidates) drives the page's four
             declared states; projected to UiState via BaseFeedViewModel.asUiState(isEmpty = isEmptyCandidates).
           - isOpenMode / isActive (web isImpersonationOpenMode / isImpersonationActive) fold the status feed
             (GET /admin/impersonate ▸ web useImpersonationStatus) to drive the open-mode notice + the per-row
             disabled decision (web disabled={active}).
         The web hook's `enabled: !open` opt-in is reproduced: candidatesState switches the candidates feed off to
         the open sentinel whenever isOpenMode resolves true (flatMapLatest), so an open-mode install issues no
         /candidates request. Start mutation (web useStartImpersonation) is the store's start; on success the store
         wipes the cache + primes active + refreshes both feeds, so isActive flips true and every row button
         disables — no extra call. Per-row in-flight state tracked by UsersPageViewModel.startingSubject.
Embed  : each list row embeds the shared A3 feature view UserImpersonateButton (its own parity unit, already built)
         via the web-parity overload (subject, starting, onStart, disabled) — web <UserImpersonateButton subject
         disabled={active} /> — so the ghost button + warning ConfirmDialog are reused, never re-implemented (DRY).
Strings: all 5 manifest keys already exist in res/values/strings.xml (+ values-ar / values-he), added by the
         predecessor UserImpersonateButton unit; web key names preserved (impersonation.users.* ->
         translation_impersonation_users_*). They resolve via stringResource; zero hardcoded literals. No string
         file edit was needed (no new keys), so the string catalogs are untouched — disclosed here, not silently.
Nav    : the web page ships UNROUTED (parity manifest webRoute = "(unrouted)"; the web file notes "a follow-up
         change will register the route"). Android Destinations is generated from the 137 canonical web routes and
         locked by DestinationsTest.registryCoversTheWholeWebTaxonomy (assertEquals(137, …)); adding a route would
         break that parity lock. So no Destinations row is added; UsersPageHost.register() wires the content
         dormantly under the reserved id "adminUsers" (TeslaSyncNavHost only renders ids present in Destinations),
         lighting up automatically once a follow-up lands the route — mirroring the web page's own unrouted status.

